vo_avfoundation: stamp tvOS host frames where both layers pick one refresh

The video layer and the OSD layer each pick the refresh that shows a
sample from its stamp. With the Apple TV's refresh matched to the frame
rate their cut-offs differ and wander: frames stamped 73-89% of the way
from one vsync to the next showed their subtitles a refresh before or
after the picture, for a whole stretch of playback or every few frames. A
host stamp is mpv's target time, so where it falls in the refresh is set
anew at every pause and seek and kept until the next: whether playback
desynced, and which way, looked random, and a pause or seek changed it
(plezy#3073).

On tvOS a display link now follows the display's vsyncs, and a host stamp
moves to the nearest point 30% of the way through a refresh interval, the
farthest from those cut-offs, by at most half a refresh either way; on
average a frame shows on the refresh it showed on before. Android's
ExoPlayer places frames the same way (VideoFrameReleaseHelper: the
nearest vsync, released 80% of a refresh before it). iOS and macOS track
no vsync and keep their stamps.

Apple TV 4K at 2160p 23.976 Hz, Plezy's options, the mode switch made
after the first frame as Plezy makes it; a 23.976 fps clip whose frames
and per-frame ASS events carry the same code in both halves of the
picture, captured over HDMI, a pause or a seek every 3 s. Of 143
stretches at the stamps mpv gave, every mismatched frame came from the
37 at 70-90% of the interval: 354 of 9576 frames, 5 stretches mismatched
for most of their length (the worst 66 of 67 frames, subtitles a refresh
late). Stamps forced to 80%: 11 of 40 stretches mismatched, 3 of them
throughout. At 30% (105 stretches, this patch and the same stamps
forced): 28 of 6615 frames, none of a stretch's for long (the worst 10
of 69), each a single refresh where one layer, mostly the video, kept a
frame a refresh longer.

